{"encoding":"verislop.autonomous-critique/0.1","verdict":"REPAIR","counterexamples":[],"corrections":[{"diagnostic_index":0,"artifact":"formalization.json","explanation":"O1 is bound to VeriSlopAST.solve_output_structure, whose formula is the constant True. This is not an executable guarantee about the admitted implementation symbol VeriSlopAST.solve. Replace the O1 binding with a theorem that states the observable output structure of solve, e.g. that for all inputs, solve(x) equals Output.mk (the filtered-and-projected rows) (the summed amounts) (the count), or at minimum that the three projections rows/total/count are well-defined and match the definition. The current constant-True predicate cannot constrain any implementation and triggers the missing_implementation_symbol diagnostic."},{"diagnostic_index":1,"artifact":"formalization.json","explanation":"O1's guarantee is structurally tautological (True). The formalization must state a non-trivial observable property of VeriSlopAST.solve, such as: ∀ x : Input, solve x = Output.mk (List.map (fun r => OutputRow.mk r.tag r.amount) (List.filter (fun r => r.enabled = true ∧ r.amount ≤ x.minimum) x.rows)) (List.sum (List.map (fun r => r.amount) (List.filter (fun r => r.enabled = true ∧ r.amount ≤ x.minimum) x.rows))) (List.length (List.filter (fun r => r.enabled = true ∧ r.amount ≤ x.minimum) x.rows)). This is already proven by rfl in _vs_body_solve, so bind O1 to that theorem or a projection thereof."}]}